 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The number of steps is proportional to the number of voxels traversed by the ray. In the worst case (ray passing through the entire volume), it is $O(N_x + N_y + N_z)$, but typically much less due to early exit on hit.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory for traversal state, plus the storage for the 3D texture.
+*   **Time Complexity:** The number of steps is proportional to the number of voxels traversed by the ray [G]. In the worst case (ray passing through the entire volume), it is $O(N_x + N_y + N_z)$, but typically much less due to early exit on hit [G].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory for traversal state, plus the storage for the 3D texture [G].
 *   **Performance:** The method is highly efficient because it minimizes floating-point operations by precomputing deltas and using integer arithmetic for index updates [S2, unofficial]. It avoids the overhead of complex acceleration structures like BVHs or KD-trees, making it suitable for real-time rendering on GPUs [S2, unofficial].
 *   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, low memory overhead, fast traversal for uniform voxel grids.
-*   **Limitations:** Performance degrades with very high resolution grids if the ray traverses many empty voxels (though early exit helps). It assumes a uniform voxel grid. It does not handle non-axis-aligned volumes or complex geometry directly.
-*   **When to use:** Real-time rendering of voxel-based data (e.g., medical imaging, voxel art) where the grid is uniform and axis-aligned.
-*   **When not to use:** Non-uniform grids, complex implicit surfaces, or scenes requiring hierarchical acceleration.
